## Proposed repair plan
# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:`<startup>`

## 1. Diagnosis — why the set fails

The failing "test" is not a test. `<startup>` is the harness's synthetic id for
"the suite never got as far as collecting tests". The discovery artefact
`tmp/{{RUN_DIR}}/testrun-001-playwright-discover-stdout.json`
shows exactly that:

```
"suites": [],
"errors": [{ "message": "Error: Process from config.webServer was not able to start. Exit code: 1" }],
"stats": { "duration": 1097.946, "expected": 0, ... }
```

`tests/playwright.config.ts` boots the app under test with:

```ts
webServer: {
  command: 'docker compose up --build frontend',
  cwd: '..',
  url: `http://127.0.0.1:${port}`,          // port derived from cwd hash, e.g. 23521
  env: { FRONTEND_PORT: String(port), COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: project },
  timeout: 300_000,
  reuseExistingServer: true,
}
```

and `docker-compose.yaml` declares:

```yaml
services:
  frontend:
    build:
      context: ./services/frontend
    ports:
      - "${FRONTEND_PORT:-3000}:3000"
```

**`services/` does not exist in this repository at all.** Verified with
`Glob("services/**")` → "No files found". So `docker compose up --build frontend`
fails in ~1 second with exit code 1 (cannot resolve the build context /
no Dockerfile), Playwright aborts before collection, and the whole suite is
reported as the single `<startup>` failure.

This is not a bug in existing production code — the production service has
never been written. `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares it as
expected, and `CLAUDE.md` confirms it:

```yaml
services:
  - name: frontend
    path: services/frontend
    language: typescript
    frameworks: [next.js, react]
```

> "Production source lives under `services/`; the frontend Next.js service is
> expected at `services/frontend/`."

**The repair is therefore a greenfield build: create the `services/frontend`
Next.js App Router service so that the compose build succeeds and the app
serves the home page.**

## 2. Scope / guardrails

